<!--
    kuna issue #762 (P6 variables, naming-policy; P9 emission, declaration
    emission).  Two distinct pointer locals both took the name `object` from
    the parameter of the callee they are passed to, and `dedupvardecls` then
    collapsed their two identical declarations into one, so the emitted C read
    and wrote one variable where the binary keeps two.  The C compiles, but the
    store after the inner loop hits the last member instead of the retained
    representative.

    visit/consume (0x1100, original synthetic C, gcc 13.3 -O2 -fPIC, consume in
    its own translation unit):

      struct Node { unsigned char skip; unsigned int hits; };
      void consume(struct Node *object) { ++object->hits; }
      void visit(struct Node **representatives, unsigned int representative_count,
                 struct Node **members, unsigned int member_count) {
          for (unsigned int i = 0; i < representative_count; ++i) {
              struct Node *representative = representatives[i];
              consume(representative);
              representative->skip = 1;
              for (unsigned int j = 0; j < member_count; ++j) {
                  struct Node *member = members[j];
                  if (!member) break;
                  if (!member->skip) consume(member);
              }
              representative->skip = 0;
          }
      }

    The representative lives in r12 across the inner loop, the member in rdi.
    Before the fix every pass printed `object = *v2;` inside the loop and one
    `Node *object;` declaration, so `object->skip = 0` stored through the member.

    The rest (0x401000, gcc 11.4 -O2 -fno-ipa-ra -fno-inline, static non-PIE) is
    the same defect reached other ways, plus two globals a renamed local must
    not capture:

      struct P { long a; long b; };
      struct Item { unsigned char skip; unsigned int hits; };
      struct P obj = { 100, 200 };
      struct Item node[8];
      void take(struct P pt);  void put(struct P obj, long k);  void touchl(long *q);
      void release(struct Item *node);
      void k1(struct P *px, long c, long d) {
          struct P x = *px; struct P y = { c * 7, d * 11 }; take(y); take(x); }
      long m1(long c, long d) {
          struct P y = { c * 7, d * 11 }; put(y, 1); return obj.a + obj.b; }
      long m2(long c, long d) {
          struct P y = { c * 7, d * 11 }; put(y, 1);
          struct P z = { c * 3, d * 5 }; put(z, 2); return obj.a + obj.b; }
      long s4(long n) {
          long p = n * 3; touchl(&p); long q = n * 5; touchl(&q); return q * 100 + p; }
      void gc(struct Item **reps, unsigned n) {
          for (unsigned i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
              struct Item *r = reps[i]; release(r); node[i & 7].hits += 3; r->skip = 1; } }

    stackreturn (0x100020) is tests/datatests/stackreturn.xml with its stack
    slot mapped under the name of the global `perf_ret`.

    k1: `y` is built from registers for the call, so its fields are pieces of
    one root, and `x` had already taken `take`'s name `pt`; the root reused it
    and `y`'s field stores printed as stores into `x`.  m1, m2: the local takes
    `put`'s name `obj`, which the global owns, so its declaration is renamed
    `obj_1`, but the field stores (pieces with no declaration of their own) and
    the second call's argument (collapsed onto the first) kept `obj` and wrote
    the global.  s4: the script renames both stack slots to `w`, so two slots
    share a name, the second is declared `w_1`, but `&v2` (a reference, not a
    declaration) kept printing `&w`; under Ghidra names, which carry no storage
    comment, the two slots also printed identical `int8 w;` lines and were
    collapsed into one variable.  Renaming only `v2` to `v1` must not collide at
    all: the untouched slot's default name skips the one the rename locked, so
    the caller's `v1` stays on the slot it named.  gc: the local takes `release`'s name `node`
    and is declared `node_1`; the `node[...]` array accesses are references to
    the global and must keep its name.  stackreturn: the slot is declared
    `perf_ret_1`, and its 2- and 4-byte views must follow it rather than read
    the global.

    The naming fix: upstream gives every named high its own local Symbol, so
    `lookForFuncParamNames`' `makeNameUnique` sees the first `object` and the
    second becomes `object_00`.  kuna binds these names on the HighVariable
    without a Symbol, so the uniqueness check now also consults the names
    already bound in the pass, and a struct root only shares its name with other
    roots.  The declaration fix: the identical-line collapse only merges lines
    of one object (one bound or referenced Symbol), and every high printed under
    another's declaration (a piece, a collapsed duplicate, an `&symbol`
    reference) follows that declaration's final name.  Neither is a judgment
    call, so there is no option; `dedupvardecls off` must print the same code.

    Pass 1 (default), pass 2 (option dedupvardecls off) and pass 3 (option
    namestyle ghidra) all print every function correctly.
-->
